{
  "timestamp": "2026-08-11T19:28:24Z",
  "title": "Harvard Alumni in Tech Everywhere, in Crimson Pro and Inter",
  "type": "feature",
  "userRequest": "Project context: We need to modernize the https://www.harvardintech.com/ website from the outside. The site was built with a no-code product so we need to faithfully reproduce it using modern technologies and host it as a GitHub pages website. It also needs a CRM that can be run locally or, ideally also in the cloud via GitHub pages as well (protected with a password and/or GitHub OAuth). Can we dig into this?",
  "description": "Two changes that touch nearly every page. The organisation is now called Harvard Alumni in Tech in copy everywhere — 57 files across content collections, page and component copy, media.json alt text, the SEO description fallbacks on both [slug] routes, the isolated-component demo props, the internal docs, the frozen design-review artifacts, and the six test files whose fixtures would otherwise have drifted from reality. The `HIT` abbreviation was expanded in the same pass. Three things were deliberately left alone because they are identifiers rather than copy: the harvardintech.com domain and contact address, the two event filenames that become URLs, and the medium.com/harvard-in-tech publication URLs — which does mean the prose now names a Medium publication whose handle still reads harvard-in-tech, worth renaming on Medium's side. Typography moved to Crimson Pro for display and Inter for body, loaded in one stylesheet request beside the JetBrains Mono that already set the kickers and utility bar. These are open-licensed faces chosen for Harvard's serif-forward character, not Harvard's licensed identity typefaces, which are neither distributable nor obviously an independent alumni group's to use; every consumer reads a token, so licensed files can be swapped in place. `--font-serif` moved off Times onto the same serif, which is what turns the Luma embeds, the volunteer hero and the volunteer project headings from a third unplanned voice into a deliberate one. The rename surfaced a real regression. The chapter hero set its title at a flat 58px with `white-space: nowrap`, so once the organisation name grew by seven characters 'HARVARD ALUMNI IN TECH SEATTLE / PACIFIC NORTHWEST' ran off BOTH edges of a 1440px viewport with its flanking rules pushed off-screen — nowrap had nothing to give. The title is now fluid via clamp() and allowed to wrap, and `.chapter-hero-inner` grew from a flat 918px to min(1280px, 94vw): that 918px had never been load-bearing while nowrap simply overflowed it, and the moment wrapping was allowed it started breaking 'NEW / YORK CITY' mid-phrase. The tagline re-applies 918px so it keeps a reading measure. A new application scenario, chapter-route-longest-chapter-name-wraps, pins the boundary case. One workflow note worth carrying: content and data edits stayed invisible in the preview until the .codeyam/tmp/content-sandbox-active snapshot was cleared, so the rename was verified by curling the served pages rather than by trusting a source grep.",
